WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.899
It's International Women's Day and we're
here to celebrate the cultural, economic

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:08.730
and social achievements of women around
the world. I've got one amazing woman

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.500
here with me - this is Patricia Bright she
is the new cover girl for GLAMOUR and

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.090
we're here to talk about all things
International Women's Day. I'm really

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:23.100
excited, as well, I love the idea of just
empowering and supporting women even

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.689
just by telling my own story. Tell me, has
anybody really helped you on the ladder

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:31.199
to success today is there one woman that
particularly stands out who wasn't a

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:35.610
member of your family? I would say I've
got loads of inspirations in this space

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:40.829
of the internet, YouTube and there's a
lady called Marie Forleo who's kind of

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:45.360
like a motivational speaker she has this
amazing course and I remember her just

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.820
sharing her story and just being really
inspired by her in this digital space. As

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.539
a young black woman growing up in the UK
today did you feel the opportunities

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:59.100
were open to you or did you feel you
often faced brick walls and closures? I

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:05.189
felt like things would be maybe a touch
harder for me because 1. woman, 2.

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:10.080
black - that not necessarily everyone
would know where I came from or

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.080
necessarily relate to me but you know I
was lucky enough to be part of two

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.460
things. When I got my first job the
way I ended up getting that job was by

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.900
attending something called the Women in
Technology Dinner and I was also part of

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.180
a community called
SEO which was to get black people into

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.560
all the top investment banks, all the top
law firms and all the top kind of

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.990
financial institutions so I had the
backing to get myself into those circles

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:44.670
so that whatever limitations there were
that I could at least try and be part of

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.020
a community who would support me through
them, but the fact that I needed to be

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.950
part of things like that to get the
roles obviously shows that there were

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.210
some barriers, but I was happy that there
are opportunities like that for me. Who

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:02.280
told you about these opportunities? Very
hard to know because growing up I didn't

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.060
know so I first worked in investment
banking before I started becoming a

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.319
YouTuber and I studied accounting and I
met a

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:15.599
guy who was on a finance course and he
got a job at a ban and he's like "there's

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:19.920
really good opportunities, check out the
website" for one of the first films that

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.249
I worked for and there was an
opportunity to come for this Women in

00:02:23.249 --> 00:02:26.879
Technology Dinner. What do you think the
biggest barrier is for young women today

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:31.019
who are trying to succeed in the
workplace? I think one of the barriers is

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:35.340
sometimes not believing that you can
necessarily do it. I think it's really

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.510
important for girls to know that you can
break through it and you can bring your

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.790
feminine energy to a space and actually
make it better. Do you think that maybe

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.269
when you say bring your feminist energy
do you think that women feel they can't

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:52.469
be themselves? Do you think they feel
that they need to be maybe or reserved

00:02:52.469 --> 00:02:56.909
or more masculine in some way? I do feel
like people feel like they have to go

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:01.500
one way or the other - so I'm quite a
dominant woman sometimes I'm like "oh

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.370
maybe I should be a bit more girly" and
then there are some who are a bit more

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.120
maybe reserved and they think maybe they
need to change themselves but I think

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.699
that, every individual the best way to be
successful is to be your best version of

00:03:14.699 --> 00:03:17.280
yourself.
There isn't an issue with it you just

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.389
have to kind of own that and share it
with the teams or the people that you

00:03:21.389 --> 00:03:25.829
work with that this is the way you are
and they can adapt themselves to work

00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:28.859
with you. Can you think of a time that
you've made a crack in your glass

00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:32.639
ceiling that you feel particularly proud
of? I feel like I'm doing that now with

00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:38.009
my own YouTube channel and just kind of
creating my own space and my own

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:41.819
businesses and I've worked with
different management companies and

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:46.919
agencies in the past which often were
run by men but, you know, I decided to

00:03:46.919 --> 00:03:51.720
kind of bring a lot of stuff in-house
and partner and employ more women and

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.590
more girls - not for positive
discrimination but just because I

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.540
connect with them as one I feel like the
fact that I'm able to do that and be in

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.500
that position is breaking a glass
ceiling. Have you ever had a time where

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.060
you faced discrimination at work and how
did you deal with it? I'm not sure if it

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:15.209
was that I felt that it was specifically
discrimination, maybe feeling a little

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:19.500
bit left out as I keep mentioning I was
really in a male dominated

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.690
environment and it's like, don't really
know how to get involved you know you're

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.070
talking about the football or your
favorite beers and that kind of stuff

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:33.840
and I'm just like I like lipstick but
I'm not gonna have that conversation. For

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.860
me, overcoming it was kind of having to
just get involved like when they went

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.040
down to the pub I would join them at the
pub and have a beer. Can you think over

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.380
time as a woman that you've triumphed over
tragedy?

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.540
I wouldn't say triumphed over tragedy I
just say triumphed - you know childbirth!

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:58.620
Being a mum I feel like that to me is a
triumph and you know being self-employed

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.740
as well and a mum and having to combine
the two. How do you juggle it all how do

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:09.890
you make it all work? It's not easy it is
quite hard to make it work I think

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:15.780
routine,  order and just you know being
that multitasking woman is the way to

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:21.150
make it work. What does feminism mean to
you today? For me personally feminism

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.840
means appreciating the difference
between men and women.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.860
I feel fundamentally we're very
different and the values that women have

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:34.290
are extremely extraordinary in my
opinion and it should be on the same

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.800
level and we should be treated in the
same way that men are even though we are

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.610
different. What hopes and dreams do you
have for women, for your daughter in the

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:51.090
future? I hope that women will feel
confident to embody themselves as they

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:55.710
are and that they don't have to change
or adapt or be more one way or more

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:01.530
another way to fit in or to get what
they actually deserve as a human being

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.540
in life. So for more videos like this and
if you want to see what happened behind

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:11.970
the scenes of my cover shoot make sure
you subscribe to the GLAMOUR UK YouTube

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.330
channel.

